Chapter 1 — Why Git Exists
Imagine This

You write a 50-page handbook.

Tomorrow you accidentally delete Chapter 4.

Gone.

No backup.

Three months of work disappears.

Git was invented to solve exactly this problem.

It remembers every meaningful version of your project.

Think of Git as a time machine for your work.

Not a cloud drive.

Not a backup service.

A history engine.

Note to Write

Git records the evolution of a project instead of simply storing the latest version.

Chapter 2 — Git vs GitHub

This confuses almost everyone.

Git

Git is software.

It lives on your computer.

It tracks changes.

It doesn't require the internet.

Git is the engine.

GitHub

GitHub is a website.

It stores Git repositories.

It allows collaboration.

It hosts Pull Requests.

It stores Issues.

It shows commit history.

GitHub is built around Git.

Good Analogy

Think of writing a Word document.

Microsoft Word = Git

OneDrive = GitHub

Word edits the file.

OneDrive stores and shares it.

Note to Write

Git is version control software.

GitHub is a hosting and collaboration platform built around Git.

Chapter 3 — Repository

A repository (repo) is the complete project.

Not just today's files.

Everything.

Including:

Every commit
Every branch
Every tag
Every release
Every version

Project Ascent is one repository.

Note

A repository contains both the project and its complete history.

Chapter 4 — Commits

This is where most beginners misunderstand Git.

A commit is not just "saving."

Think of it as a checkpoint in the story of your project.

Imagine writing a book.

You wouldn't save every keystroke forever.

Instead, you'd say:

"Chapter 2 is complete."

That's a commit.

Every commit has:

an ID (SHA)
an author
a timestamp
a message
the exact changes

Example:

Commit

7f72c6e

Author:
Royal

Message:
Complete Mission 001 Atlas Core

That SHA is unique.

No one else will ever produce that exact commit.

Notes

A commit is an intentional checkpoint.

Every commit tells part of the project's story.

Chapter 5 — Branches

This is one of the most important concepts.

Imagine Project Ascent is a tree.

main

That's the trunk.

You don't experiment on the trunk.

Instead...

You grow a branch.

main
    │
    ├── atlas-mission-001

Everything happens there.

If you make mistakes...

No problem.

Main is untouched.

When the work is complete...

Merge.

