Notes

Branches isolate work.

They allow experimentation without risking the main project.

Chapter 6 — Staging

This is probably Git's strangest concept.

Git has three major areas:

Working Directory

↓

Staging Area

↓

Repository

Imagine packing a suitcase.

Working directory:

Everything in your room.

Staging:

The clothes you've decided to pack.

Commit:

The suitcase is zipped.

Commands
git add README.md

Moves changes into staging.

git add .

Stages everything.

Notes

The staging area lets me decide exactly what belongs in the next commit.

Chapter 7 — Pull Requests

A Pull Request is not Git.

It's GitHub.

A PR says:

"I'd like these branch changes reviewed before they become part of main."

Think of it like submitting a research paper.

You don't publish immediately.

Someone reviews it.

Project Ascent workflow:

Royal

↓

Create Branch

↓

Commit

↓

Push

↓

Draft PR

↓

Review

↓

Merge

Sound familiar?

You already did it.

Notes

Pull Requests encourage discussion before changes become permanent.

Chapter 8 — Merge

Merging combines histories.

main

↓

Branch

↓

Merge

↓

Updated main

Nothing magical.

Git simply applies the approved history.

Chapter 9 — Status

Atlas's Golden Rule

Before doing anything:

git status

After doing anything:

git status

Before committing:

git status

After committing:

git status

You cannot overuse this command.

Why?

Because Git tells you exactly what it believes.

If Git thinks something is wrong...

Stop.

Read.

Don't guess.

Chapter 10 — Diff

Before every commit:

git diff

Git shows:

Exactly

What

Changed.

No surprises.

Professional developers check diffs constantly.

